<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ headline.claim }} — pl-review-sense</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ headline.detail }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ headline.claim }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ headline.detail }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="https://p0w3r223.github.io/pl-review-sense/">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
<link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22 viewBox=%220 0 16 16%22><text y=%2213%22 font-size=%2213%22>&#128172;</text></svg>">
<style>
{{ styles }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">Polish review sentiment · PolEmo 2.0 · {{ manifest.splits.test }} test reviews</p>
  <h1>{{ headline.claim }}</h1>
  <p class="lead">{{ headline.detail }}</p>
</header>

<ul class="kpis">
  {% for kpi in kpis %}
  <li class="kpi">
    <div class="kpi-value">{{ kpi.value }}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">{{ kpi.label }}</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">{{ kpi.note }}</div>
  </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>

<h2>What the corpus score is, and how tightly it is pinned</h2>
<p>
  Macro-F1 rather than accuracy, because the classes are imbalanced: answering
  <em>negative</em> to everything would score respectably on accuracy and badly here. The
  figure below it is the one that is usually left off — a score on
  {{ manifest.splits.test }} reviews is a sample, and the interval says how much of the third
  decimal is real.
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>class</th><th class="num">precision</th><th class="num">recall</th>
      <th class="num">F1</th><th class="num">test reviews</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for row in baseline.per_class %}
    <tr>
      <th>{{ row.label }}</th>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(row.precision) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(row.recall) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(row.f1) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ row.support }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% if interval %}
<p class="note">
  Macro-F1 <strong>{{ '%.3f' | format(interval.macro_f1) }}</strong>, and
  {{ '%.0f%%' | format(interval.confidence * 100) }} of
  {{ interval.resamples }} bootstrap resamples of the test rows fall between
  {{ '%.3f' | format(interval.low) }} and {{ '%.3f' | format(interval.high) }}. Any comparison
  narrower than {{ '%.3f' | format(interval.high - interval.low) }} is a comparison this test
  set cannot make.
</p>
{% endif %}
{% if floors %}
<p class="note">
  What that is worth depends on what it is read against, so the floors are on the page rather
  than left to the reader's intuition:
  {% for floor in floors %}<strong>{{ floor.name }}</strong> scores
  {{ '%.3f' | format(floor.macro_f1) }} macro-F1 at
  {{ '%.3f' | format(floor.accuracy) }} accuracy{% if not loop.last %}, {% else %}.{% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
  The first of those is the whole argument for the headline metric: it is a model that has
  learned nothing, and accuracy still flatters it.
</p>
{% endif %}
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.confusion }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    Rows are the true class, columns what the model answered. Shading is the share of each
    row, so the three classes are comparable despite differing threefold in size. The
    confusions are almost entirely between the two ends of the scale, not with the middle.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Is the transformer better?</h2>
{% if mcnemar and herbert %}
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>model</th><th class="num">accuracy</th><th class="num">macro-F1</th><th class="num">interval</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>TF-IDF + logistic regression</th>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(baseline.accuracy) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(baseline.macro_f1) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(interval.low) }}–{{ '%.3f' | format(interval.high) }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>HerBERT, fine-tuned</th>
      <td class="num">{% if herbert_metrics %}{{ '%.3f' | format(herbert_metrics.accuracy) }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(herbert.macro_f1) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(herbert.low) }}–{{ '%.3f' | format(herbert.high) }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  The intervals are not the comparison. Both models answer the same
  {{ manifest.splits.test }} reviews, so the question is which reviews they disagree on:
  HerBERT is right on <strong>{{ mcnemar.only_herbert_correct }}</strong> the baseline misses,
  the baseline is right on <strong>{{ mcnemar.only_baseline_correct }}</strong> HerBERT misses,
  and the remaining {{ manifest.splits.test - mcnemar.discordant }} they answer alike. An exact
  McNemar test on those {{ mcnemar.discordant }} disagreements gives
  <strong>p = {{ '%.4f' | format(mcnemar.p_value) }}</strong>.
</p>
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <p>
    <strong>Waiting for a GPU run.</strong> HerBERT fine-tunes on a free Colab GPU
    (<code>notebooks/herbert_colab.ipynb</code>); this repository has no representative run yet,
    so there is no second column and no comparison. The local CPU smoke run is not it — it
    fine-tunes {{ smoke_subset }} reviews for one epoch to prove the training code executes,
    and its numbers are written to a separate file that this page never reads.
  </p>
  <p>
    When that run lands, the comparison here will not be two scores side by side. Both models
    answer the same {{ manifest.splits.test }} reviews, so the evidence is which reviews they
    disagree on: an exact McNemar test over the disagreements, which can separate models whose
    intervals overlap and can refuse to separate models whose intervals do not.
  </p>
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>How much of this score is data rather than model</h2>
<p>
  Before asking what a transformer would add, it is worth asking what the corpus adds. The
  same pipeline is refit from scratch on stratified subsamples of the training split and
  scored on the untouched test split every time.
</p>
{% if curve and curve.points %}
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.curve }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    Macro-F1 against the number of labelled reviews, on a log axis because the sizes double.
    The band is the spread over {{ curve.seeds | length }} draws at each size — where it is
    wide, the point is a draw of the dice rather than a property of the size. The y axis
    starts above zero; the floor is printed on it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
{% if curve_within %}
<p>
  <strong>{{ curve_within.size }} labelled reviews</strong> —
  {{ '%.0f%%' | format(curve_within.share_of_corpus * 100) }} of the corpus — already land
  within {{ '%.3f' | format(curve_within.gap) }} macro-F1 of what all
  {{ curve.points[-1].size }} produce. The remaining
  {{ curve.points[-1].size - curve_within.size }} annotations buy the last
  {{ '%.1f' | format(curve_within.gap * 100) }} points. Whether that is worth paying for is a
  budget question, but it is the same question as "should we fine-tune a transformer", asked
  where the answer is cheaper to test.
</p>
{% endif %}
{% if cost %}
<p class="note">
  What the cheap side of that trade costs, measured rather than asserted: the full pipeline
  fits in <strong>{{ '%.1f' | format(cost.train_seconds) }} s</strong> on
  {{ cost.machine }} ({{ cost.device }}),
  {% if cost.model_bytes %}the saved model is
  {{ '%.1f' | format(cost.model_bytes / 1000000) }} MB, and it classifies
  {% else %}and it classifies {% endif %}
  {{ '%.0f' | format(cost.predict_rows_per_second) }} reviews a second. HerBERT's side of the comparison is a GPU and minutes per epoch, and it stays a
  description rather than a number until that run happens.
</p>
{% endif %}
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <strong>Not computed yet.</strong> Run <code>python -m pl_review_sense.analysis</code> to
  produce the curve from the committed corpus.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>Where a bag of words slips</h2>
<p>
  The corpus score says how the model does on reviews like the ones it was trained on. It says
  nothing about the phenomena a lexical model is supposed to be bad at. This section used to
  assert that negation and irony are its weak points and illustrate the claim with three
  unscored sentences; it now measures it.
</p>
{% if probe %}
<p>
  {{ probe.base_cases }} sentences, written for this page in the four domains PolEmo draws
  from, plus {{ probe.derived_cases }} derived by re-spelling the same sentences — diacritics
  stripped, and one adjacent-character swap in the longest word. No PolEmo text appears here;
  the corpus is non-commercial and is not redistributed by this repository.
</p>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.probe }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    The track is the size of each cell, the bar what was answered correctly. Read
    <code>plain</code> first: it is the control, unambiguous sentiment with no negation, irony
    or pivot, and everything below it is only interesting relative to that. The two derived
    cells re-score the same sentences, so they belong beside the base cells rather than added
    to them.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>cell</th><th>what it holds</th><th class="num">correct</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for row in probe.scores %}
    <tr>
      <th>{{ row.phenomenon }}</th>
      <td>{{ row.note }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ row.correct }} / {{ row.total }}{% if row.thin %} <span class="hint">below the floor for a rate</span>{% endif %}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  The cells are built so that no single answer can win them. Every ironic sentence has a
  genuine counterpart in the same enthusiastic register, down to minimal pairs — "bateria
  wytrzymała całe trzy godziny" against "całe trzy dni" — because a probe made only of ironic
  praise would be scored 20 of 20 by a model that answers <em>negative</em> to everything. The
  negation cell carries cues in both directions for the same reason.
</p>
{% if probe.misses %}
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>sentence</th><th>cell</th><th>should be</th><th>answered</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for miss in probe.misses %}
    <tr>
      <td class="quote">{{ miss.text }}</td>
      <td>{{ miss.phenomenon }}</td>
      <td>{{ miss.gold }}</td>
      <td>{{ miss.predicted }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="note">
  {{ probe.misses | length }} of {{ probe.misses_total }} misses, in the order they were
  scored rather than picked for effect.
</p>
{% endif %}
{% if probe.directions %}
<h3>Where the misses go</h3>
<p>
  A count of correct answers cannot tell a model that misreads irony apart from one that
  answers <em>negative</em> to everything — both score the same. The direction of the errors
  can, which is why it is a column rather than a sentence.
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>cell</th><th>should be</th><th>answered</th><th class="num">times</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for move in probe.directions[:8] %}
    <tr>
      <th>{{ move.phenomenon }}</th>
      <td>{{ move.gold }}</td>
      <td>{{ move.predicted }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ move.count }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="note">
  The eight most frequent of {{ probe.directions | length }} distinct
  should-be/answered pairs, over all {{ probe.misses_total }} misses including the derived
  cells.
</p>
{% endif %}
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <strong>Not scored yet.</strong> Run <code>python -m pl_review_sense.analysis</code>.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>Is that about length, or about our sentences?</h2>
<p>
  The section above is the page's own claim, and it rests on sentences we wrote. The obvious
  objection is that the probe measures our writing rather than its length. PolEmo is where to
  put that question: nobody here chose its reviews, and the same predictions that produced the
  headline score can be cut by how long each one is. Whether the corpus is in a position to
  answer is the first thing the cut has to establish.
</p>
{% if length and length.segments %}
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.length }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    The same {{ manifest.splits.test }} test reviews, grouped by word count. Macro-F1 inside a
    group averages only the classes that occur in it — a group holding no neutral reviews would
    otherwise take a hard zero for that class and report its own composition as the model's
    failure. Groups under {{ length.min_segment_n }} reviews are muted: a direction, not a score.
    The median review in this split is {{ length.median_length }} words, and the shortest is
    {{ length.shortest }}.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<p>
  {% if length_reading == 'unanswerable' %}
  <strong>The corpus cannot settle it.</strong> PolEmo is made of written reviews — the median
  one in this split is {{ length.median_length }} words — and only
  {{ length.rows_at_probe_scale }} of its {{ manifest.splits.test }} test reviews are as short
  as the probe's sentences ({{ length.probe_median_length }} words at the median). The length
  the claim is about is a length this corpus barely contains, which is why the sentences had to
  be written rather than sampled.
  {% if length.trend is not none %}
  Across the lengths it does cover, counting only groups of at least
  {{ length.min_segment_n }} reviews, the score moves by
  {{ '%.3f' | format(length.trend) }} macro-F1 between the shortest and the longest of them —
  inside the interval around the score itself, so not a direction at all.
  {% endif %}
  So the honest reading of the section above is narrower than "short text breaks it": on
  sentence-length Polish reviews of the kind this corpus does not contain, the model is not the
  0.94 model. What separates the two — length, register, or the distance between a review and a
  constructed sentence — this page cannot take apart, and does not claim to.
  {% elif length_reading == 'corroborates' %}
  The corpus says the same thing as the probe: macro-F1 is
  {{ '%.3f' | format(length.trend) }} higher on the longest reviews than on the shortest, and
  the model that scores {{ '%.3f' | format(baseline.macro_f1) }} overall is not scoring that on
  short text. Two independent pieces of evidence — one written for this page, one not.
  {% elif length_reading == 'contradicts' %}
  The corpus disagrees with the probe: the score is {{ '%.3f' | format(0 - length.trend) }}
  <em>higher</em> on the shortest reviews than on the longest. Whatever the probe is measuring,
  it is not length — register, or the distance between a written review and a constructed
  sentence, are the candidates. The headline above is about the probe, and only about it.
  {% elif length_reading == 'climbs' %}
  The score climbs {{ '%.3f' | format(length.trend) }} macro-F1 from the shortest reviews to
  the longest, so length does cost this model something — even though the probe above did not
  fall far enough for the page to lead with it.
  {% else %}
  Length turns out to explain little: the shortest and longest groups are within
  {{ '%.3f' | format(within_delta) }} macro-F1 of each other. So the probe's result is not a
  length effect, and the page cannot claim it is. What separates a PolEmo review from one of
  our sentences is register and construction, not word count — which is a narrower finding
  than "short text breaks it", and the one the evidence supports.
  {% endif %}
</p>
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <strong>Not computed yet.</strong> Run <code>python -m pl_review_sense.analysis</code>; the
  cut needs the corpus, which is downloaded on demand rather than committed.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>What the model keys on</h2>
{% if terms %}
<p>
  A linear model has two advantages over a transformer, and only one of them is the compute
  bill. These are the {{ terms.top_n }} heaviest coefficients per class — the model's own
  reasons, not an explanation reconstructed after the fact. They are weights, not review text.
</p>
<div class="columns">
  {% for label in labels %}
  <div>
    <h3>{{ label }}</h3>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts['terms_' ~ label] }}</div>
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</div>
<p class="note">
  Worth reading against the cells above. Where a class's heaviest terms are evaluative, the
  model has learned sentiment; where they are topical — the vocabulary of the domains those
  reviews happen to come from — it has learned to recognise the subject matter, and will carry
  that habit onto any sentence about the same subject. A single token weighted for one class
  also cannot be conditioned on what follows it, which is what the negation cell measures.
</p>
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <strong>Not extracted yet.</strong> Run <code>python -m pl_review_sense.analysis</code>.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>Knowing when not to answer</h2>
{% if deferral %}
<p>
  A system that can run both models does not have to choose one. The cheap model answers what
  it is sure about and hands the rest on, and the bill is then set by how much gets handed on.
  That only works if the model's confidence means something, so it is measured before it is
  used.
</p>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.reliability }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    One point per populated confidence bin: what the model claimed, against how often it was
    right. Expected calibration error
    <strong>{{ '%.3f' | format(deferral.expected_calibration_error) }}</strong>.
    {% if calibration_direction == 'under' %}
    The points sit above the diagonal — the model is <em>under</em>-confident, right more often
    than it says. That is not the harmless direction it sounds like: a rule written as "hand on
    anything below 0.7" would escalate bands this model answers perfectly.
    {% elif calibration_direction == 'over' %}
    The points sit below the diagonal — the model is <em>over</em>-confident, and a threshold
    read as a probability would keep reviews it gets wrong.
    {% else %}
    The points track the diagonal closely, so a threshold can be read as roughly the accuracy
    it buys.
    {% endif %}
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<p>
  Which is why the operating point comes from the table below rather than from the confidence
  number itself. Reviews are handed on in order of the model's own confidence, least sure
  first, and the threshold column is the lowest confidence still answered — the number that
  would go into a configuration file.
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>handed on</th><th class="num">reviews</th><th class="num">kept</th>
      <th class="num">threshold</th><th class="num">macro-F1 on what it keeps</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for point in deferral.risk_coverage %}
    <tr>
      <th>{{ '%.0f%%' | format(point.deferral_rate * 100) }}</th>
      <td class="num">{{ point.deferred }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ point.kept }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(point.threshold) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(point.macro_f1) }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="note">
  The first row is the reference: no deferral, the whole test set. That the score climbs as
  reviews are set aside is the evidence that the confidence ranks anything at all — had it
  stayed flat, no threshold built on it would work, whatever the calibration figure said.
</p>
{% if deferral.cascade %}
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>handed to HerBERT</th><th class="num">reviews</th>
      <th class="num">macro-F1, whole test set</th><th class="num">share on the GPU</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for point in deferral.cascade %}
    <tr>
      <th>{{ '%.0f%%' | format(point.deferral_rate * 100) }}</th>
      <td class="num">{{ point.deferred }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.3f' | format(point.macro_f1) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ '%.0f%%' | format(point.escalated_share * 100) }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <strong>The cascade needs the second model.</strong> The table above says what the baseline
  scores on the reviews it keeps — a statement about one model, publishable today. What the
  combined system would score depends on how HerBERT answers the reviews handed to it, and
  those predictions do not exist yet. Nothing here estimates them: a cascade figure computed
  without the expensive model is a guess wearing three decimal places.
</div>
{% endif %}
{% else %}
<div class="card pending">
  <strong>Not computed yet.</strong> Run <code>python -m pl_review_sense.analysis</code>.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>Method, and what it cannot tell you</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p>
    <strong>Data.</strong> PolEmo 2.0 (CLARIN-PL), the <code>{{ manifest.dataset.config }}</code>
    configuration, pinned at revision <code>{{ manifest.dataset.revision[:12] }}</code> so the
    loader script that runs is one that was audited. The <code>{{ manifest.dataset.dropped_class }}</code>
    class is dropped rather than folded into <em>neutral</em>: ambiguous is not a point on the
    negative–positive axis, and merging it would corrupt the class it was merged into. That
    leaves {{ manifest.splits.train }} training, {{ manifest.splits.validation }} validation and
    {{ manifest.splits.test }} test reviews.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>No leakage, and no tuning against the test split.</strong> The vectorizer is fit
    inside the pipeline, on the training fold only. Hyperparameters are fixed defaults in
    <code>config.py</code>; the validation split is deliberately not tuned against, so the test
    figures stay an estimate rather than a selection.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Limits.</strong> One corpus, of traditional written reviews from four domains —
    this is not a general Polish sentiment API, and the probe above is the evidence for that
    rather than a caveat about it. The probe itself is 80 sentences: enough to show that a gap
    exists, not to quantify it. The compute figures are measured on one CPU and quoted with
    the machine beside them. Nothing here says what HerBERT does, because HerBERT has not run.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What is committed.</strong> Numbers only — metrics, intervals, coefficients,
    timings and per-row predictions as label integers. No review text is stored in this
    repository; PolEmo is CC BY-NC-SA and is downloaded on demand.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>The configuration behind every number</h2>
<p class="note">
  Printed from the constants the code actually uses, not described. A setting that is quoted
  from <code>config.py</code> cannot drift away from the run that produced the figures above.
</p>
{% for name, block in settings.items() %}
<details>
  <summary>{{ name }}</summary>
  <pre><code>{{ block }}</code></pre>
</details>
{% endfor %}

<footer>
  <p>
    Built from metrics generated
    <time datetime="{{ generated }}">{{ generated[:10] }}</time> at commit
    <code>{{ manifest.git_sha }}</code>, scikit-learn {{ manifest.versions.scikit_learn }},
    Python {{ manifest.versions.python }} ·
    <a href="https://github.com/P0w3r223/pl-review-sense">source on GitHub</a> ·
    <a href="https://github.com/P0w3r223/pl-review-sense/blob/main/docs/model-card.md">model card</a> ·
    <a href="https://github.com/P0w3r223/pl-review-sense/blob/main/docs/research/data-and-methodology.md">methodology</a> ·
    <a href="https://github.com/P0w3r223/pl-review-sense/tree/main/docs/adr">decisions</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Every figure on this page is rebuilt from <code>reports/metrics/</code> by
    <code>python -m pl_review_sense.site</code>, and CI fails if the published page and those
    files disagree. The page cannot be edited into saying something the numbers do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Data: <a href="https://clarin-pl.eu/dspace/handle/11321/710">PolEmo 2.0</a> © CLARIN-PL,
    {{ manifest.dataset.license }}. Code MIT.
  </p>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
